Service state|systemctl status APP.service --no-pager|Summarizes a systemd service's state and recent messages.|Check whether startup failed, exited or remains active.|Loaded describes the unit definition; Active describes current state; Main PID identifies the process.|Active: failed (Result: exit-code)|Replace APP with the actual unit; read-only inspection.
Effective unit|systemctl cat APP.service|Prints the unit file and drop-in configuration.|Find overrides or unexpected startup arguments.|File headers identify each source; later applicable overrides modify the base definition.|# /etc/systemd/system/APP.service.d/override.conf|Read-only; displayed configuration may contain sensitive environment values.
Service journal|journalctl -u APP.service --since '-30 min' --no-pager|Filters journal entries by service and recent time.|Correlate startup failures with a change.|Read timestamps, process identifiers and errors together; retention can remove older evidence.|APP[123]: configuration error at line ...|Read-only; redact secrets before sharing logs.
Boot failures|journalctl -b -p err --no-pager|Shows error-priority messages from the current boot.|Narrow boot and initialization problems.|-b selects the boot and -p err includes error and more severe priorities; warnings are excluded.|timestamp host unit: error message|Read-only; inspect a broader priority range if necessary.
Failed units|systemctl --failed --no-pager|Lists units currently in failed state.|Find failed dependencies after reboot or maintenance.|UNIT names the failed object; ACTIVE and SUB describe its state.|UNIT LOAD ACTIVE SUB DESCRIPTION|Read-only; a previously recovered failure may no longer appear.
Timer schedule|systemctl list-timers --all --no-pager|Lists systemd timers and their recent and next triggers.|Diagnose jobs that run late or not at all.|NEXT and LEFT show expected scheduling; LAST and PASSED show prior activation; ACTIVATES names the target unit.|NEXT LEFT LAST PASSED UNIT ACTIVATES|Read-only; inspect the activated service's logs to verify job success.
Clock status|timedatectl status|Displays system clock, timezone and synchronization status.|Check clock assumptions during TLS or log-order problems.|System clock synchronized and NTP service are different indicators; inspect the time source for detailed offset.|System clock synchronized: yes|Read-only; command availability depends on the host's service stack.
Storage layers|lsblk -o NAME,SIZE,FSTYPE,MOUNTPOINTS|Lists block-device hierarchy and selected properties.|Distinguish disk, partition and filesystem sizes before expansion.|Indented children belong to parent devices; a filesystem can be smaller than the underlying block device.|sda ... / ; sdb ... /data|Read-only; MOUNTPOINTS support depends on util-linux version.
Mount validation|findmnt --verify --verbose|Checks filesystem-table parsing and usability assumptions.|Find fstab problems before a reboot.|Warnings and errors identify entries requiring investigation; success does not simulate every boot dependency.|Success, no errors or warnings detected|Read-only verification; visibility and device availability affect results.
SSH configuration check|sshd -t|Validates sshd configuration and host-key sanity.|Catch syntax errors before reloading remote access.|No output and zero exit status normally indicate validation success; errors name the problematic setting.|No output on success|May require elevated read access; validation alone does not prove a new login will succeed.
Log rotation preview|logrotate --debug /etc/logrotate.conf|Parses rotation policy and explains planned decisions without rotating logs.|Diagnose wrong paths, retention and rotation timing.|Look for the selected log, last rotation and whether rotation is due.|considering log /var/log/app.log|Debug mode does not update state or rotate files; output may include filesystem details.
Sudo syntax check|visudo -c|Checks installed sudoers policy syntax.|Verify privilege-policy integrity before ending an administrative session.|Parsed OK identifies accepted files; line errors identify syntax faults.|/etc/sudoers: parsed OK|Read-only check, usually requires privileged access; keep a working recovery session during policy edits.
Effective unit properties|systemctl show APP.service -p MainPID -p ActiveState -p SubState -p ExecMainStatus|Displays selected runtime properties for a systemd unit.|Read machine-oriented evidence of process state and exit status.|MainPID is the tracked process; ActiveState/SubState describe unit state. ExecMainStatus is not an HTTP health result.|ActiveState=active SubState=running MainPID=2481|Read-only; a live PID can still serve failed requests.
Boot enablement|systemctl is-enabled APP.service|Reports whether unit installation relationships enable activation.|Investigate why a manually started service does not return after boot.|enabled, disabled, static and masked have different meanings; static units may start through dependencies.|enabled|Read-only; enablement does not mean currently running.
Unit dependencies|systemctl list-dependencies APP.service --all|Lists unit dependencies known to the manager.|Inspect the activation relationships of a service.|Tree structure shows related units; activation dependencies differ from explicit ordering constraints.|APP.service ... network.target|Read-only; use unit properties to inspect ordering details when needed.
Reload manager configuration|systemctl daemon-reload|Reloads unit definitions and regenerates the manager dependency view.|Make edited unit files or drop-ins visible to systemd.|Success is normally silent; it does not itself restart the service or reload application configuration.|No output on success|Changes the manager's configuration view. Validate unit edits and use the separate intended service action afterward.
Reload application configuration|systemctl reload APP.service|Requests the unit's configured reload operation.|Activate a supported application configuration change without a full restart.|A successful job means the reload command completed; verify actual loaded configuration and service behavior.|No output on success|Mutates application state and requires unit reload support. Validate configuration before invoking.
Controlled service restart|systemctl restart APP.service|Stops and starts a unit according to its configuration.|Apply an approved change that requires a new process.|Successful exit indicates the start job completed under unit semantics; inspect logs and endpoint health afterward.|No output on success|Disrupts a workload; verify dependency impact, capacity and recovery access first.
Enable a service|systemctl enable APP.service|Creates installation links according to the unit's Install section.|Configure intended activation relationships such as boot startup.|Created symlink messages identify changed links. This command without --now does not start the unit.|Created symlink ...multi-user.target.wants/...|Mutates host activation policy; inspect the unit and intended target first.
Historical boot logs|journalctl --list-boots|Lists boots represented in the journal.|Find the boot index or ID for a failure before a reboot.|0 is the current boot; negative offsets refer to earlier retained boots. Start/end timestamps delimit retained evidence.|-1 BOOT_ID DATE DATE|Read-only; availability depends on journal persistence and retention.
Previous boot errors|journalctl -b -1 -p warning --no-pager|Reads warning-and-higher-priority entries from the previous retained boot.|Investigate why a host failed before it restarted.|-b -1 selects the prior boot; -p warning includes more severe priorities. Missing records may reflect retention limits.|kernel: device error ...|Read-only; log content may be sensitive and should be shared selectively.
Journal disk usage|journalctl --disk-usage|Reports disk space used by journal files.|Assess whether journaling contributes to filesystem pressure.|The total describes journal storage, not all logs under /var/log.|Archived and active journals take up 1.2G in the file system.|Read-only; inspect retention policy before deleting evidence.
User scheduled tasks|crontab -l|Lists the current user's cron entries.|Compare scheduler command paths, timing and environment assumptions.|Each non-comment entry begins with schedule fields followed by the command; this is not every user's schedule.|0 2 * * * /usr/local/bin/backup|Read-only; inspect the correct account. System timers and /etc/cron* are separate sources.
Time source tracking|chronyc tracking|Reports chrony's clock synchronization state and estimates.|Investigate clock drift affecting authentication or logs.|Reference ID names the source; System time and Last offset describe offsets. Leap status indicates synchronization condition.|Leap status: Normal|Read-only; applies to hosts using chronyd and should be correlated with source status.
Block device identifiers|blkid /dev/DEVICE|Probes filesystem identifiers for a selected block device.|Check UUID and filesystem type before editing mount configuration.|UUID is filesystem identity; TYPE describes the detected format. Verify the physical/logical device selection separately.|UUID="..." TYPE="ext4"|Read-only probe, often privileged; never infer that an unrecognized device is empty or safe to format.
Volume group capacity|vgs|Summarizes LVM volume groups and free physical capacity.|Determine whether a logical volume can be extended within its group.|VSize is group capacity and VFree is unallocated space; neither reports free filesystem bytes.|VG #PV #LV #SN Attr VSize VFree|Read-only; requires LVM tooling and suitable device visibility.
Logical volume layout|lvs -o lv_name,vg_name,lv_size,lv_attr,devices|Displays logical volumes, attributes and backing devices.|Map an application volume to underlying LVM storage.|LSize is block volume capacity; Attr encodes volume properties. Filesystem growth is a separate concern.|LV VG LSize Attr Devices|Read-only; special layouts such as thin pools require additional capacity checks.
Debian package inventory|dpkg-query -W -f='${Package} ${Version}\n' openssl|Queries the installed Debian package database for a package version.|Identify the package managed by the distribution before patch review.|Output contains package name and installed version; distribution backports mean upstream version alone may not establish vulnerability status.|openssl VERSION|Read-only; Debian/Ubuntu-family command. Check vendor advisories for security status.
RPM package inventory|rpm -q openssl|Queries the RPM database for an installed package.|Check the distribution-managed package version on an RPM-based host.|The returned name-version-release identifies the installed package; a not-installed result is distinct from an outdated version.|openssl-VERSION-RELEASE.ARCH|Read-only; RPM-family system required. Vendor release metadata matters for backported fixes.
Effective sudo privileges|sudo -l|Lists sudo commands permitted for the current identity.|Review practical privilege boundaries before administration or access changes.|Runas and command rules identify allowed operations; tools with shell escape or arbitrary-file features may grant broader capabilities.|(root) /usr/bin/systemctl restart app.service|Read-only privilege inspection but may prompt authentication and generate audit records. Do not assume a narrow command name means narrow impact.
